10%; \"> 639 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;width: 10%; \"> 0.469 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;width: 10%; \"> 0.499 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;width: 10%; \"> 0 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;width: 10%; \"> 1 </td>\n  </tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n`````\n:::\n:::\n\n\n---\n\n\n## Employment by Marital Status {.center}\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![Women's Employment Status by Marital Status](women_employment_files/figure-revealjs/fig-marital-1.png){#fig-marital width=1728}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n---\n\n\n## Employment by Family Structure {.center}\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![Women's Employment Status by Family Structure](women_employment_files/figure-revealjs/fig-family-1.png){#fig-family width=1728}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n---\n\n\n## Employment by Age Group {.center}\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![Women's Employment Status by Age Group](women_employment_files/figure-revealjs/fig-age-1.png){#fig-age width=1728}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n---\n\n\n## Employment by Education Level {.center}\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![Women's Employment Status by Education Level](women_employment_files/figure-revealjs/fig-edu-1.png){#fig-edu width=1728}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n---\n\n\n## Key Findings {.center}\n\n:::::: columns\n::: {.column width=\"31%\" style=\"font-size: 1.15em; line-height: 2.0; background-color: #dceeff; padding: 25px; border-radius: 10px; border-top: 5px solid #2c7fb8;\"}\n**📊 Marital Status**\n\n- Married → **14.2%** working\n- Unmarried → **25.0%** working\n- Widowed / Separated → **41.7%** working\n- Economic necessity drives work after loss of spouse\n:::\n\n::: {.column width=\"3%\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.column width=\"31%\" style=\"font-size: 1.15em; line-height: 2.0; background-color: #dceeff; padding: 25px; border-radius: 10px; border-top: 5px solid #2c7fb8;\"}\n**🏠 Family Structure**\n\n- Nuclear family → **20.1%** working\n- Joint family → **13.4%** working\n- Joint families **restrict** women's mobility and work decisions\n:::\n\n::: {.column width=\"3%\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.column width=\"31%\" style=\"font-size: 1.15em; line-height: 2.0; background-color: #dceeff; padding: 25px; border-radius: 10px; border-top: 5px solid #2c7fb8;\"}\n**📅 Age & Education**\n \n- Women aged **35–44** have highest employment (24.7%)\n- More educated women work **less** — social restrictions in slum communities\n:::\n::::::\n\n---\n\n\n## Thank You {.center}\n\n::: {style=\"font-size: 1.8em; text-align: center; margin-top: 120px; line-height: 3.0;\"}\n**Ramesh Beniwal**\n\n*Women's Employment Study | Urban Slums | 2026*\n:::\n",
